workedExamples.solution_text:
The $y$-intercept gives $c=4$. Moving left to right, the line falls $6$ units and runs $3$ units, so the rise is $-6$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{lineblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{risered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-1.5,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3)--(0,6);
\path (4,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[lineblue,thick,{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.75,5.5)--(3.5,-3);
\fill[black] (0,4) circle (1.8pt) (3,-2) circle (1.8pt);
\path (0,4) node[left] {$(0,4)$} (3,-2) node[right] {$(3,-2)$};
\draw[risered,dashed] (0,4)--(3,4)--(3,-2);
\path (1.5,4) node[above] {Run $=3$} (3,1) node[right] {Rise $=-6$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}m&=\frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}}\\&=\frac{-6}{3}\\&=-2\end{aligned}$

$y=-2x+4$

